::ENG2_U04_L02_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could a school garden support science learning? Source A: A state university agriculture extension article by a science education specialist explains classroom garden observations and was updated in 2025. Why is Source A reliable?{
=A. It is current, relevant, and written by a knowledgeable specialist.#Correct. These features support reliability and credibility.
~B. It gives no author or evidence.#Incorrect. The source includes an author role and relevant examples.
~C. It is an advertisement for tools.#Incorrect. The source is a university extension article, not an ad.
~D. It uses insults instead of evidence.#Incorrect. No loaded insult is described.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Source B says, "Only foolish schools avoid gardens." What does this wording show?{
~A. Neutral academic tone.#Incorrect. The wording is not neutral.
=B. Loaded language that suggests bias.#Correct. "Only foolish schools" attacks one side instead of providing evidence.
~C. Current data from a study.#Incorrect. The sentence gives no data.
~D. A valid measurement method.#Incorrect. No method is described.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could pre-order lunch forms reduce cafeteria waste? Source D: A school district nutrition report compares tray waste before and after a four-week pre-order lunch pilot. Why is this source valid?{
~A. It discusses garden tools.#Incorrect. That does not fit the lunch-form question.
~B. It gives only a personal opinion.#Incorrect. The source includes measured comparison.
=C. Its evidence directly matches the research question.#Correct. It compares waste before and after the pre-order pilot.
~D. It avoids all data.#Incorrect. It includes measured waste totals.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Source F is a cafeteria menu for this week. It is accurate, but it does not discuss waste reduction. How should a researcher evaluate it for the pre-order form question?{
~A. It is the best source because it lists food.#Incorrect. A menu does not answer the waste-reduction question.
~B. It is biased because all menus are emotional.#Incorrect. The issue is usefulness, not necessarily bias.
~C. It is invalid because accurate sources are never useful.#Incorrect. Accurate sources can be useful when they fit the question.
=D. It may be accurate but not useful for answering this research question.#Correct. A source must fit the inquiry, not just be accurate.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which checklist best evaluates a research source?{
~A. Color, font, and whether the title is exciting.#Incorrect. These do not prove reliability.
=B. Author credibility, evidence, relevance, date, purpose, and bias.#Correct. These features help determine whether a source can be trusted and used.
~C. Length only.#Incorrect. A long source can still be unreliable.
~D. Whether the source agrees with the writer's first opinion.#Incorrect. Research requires fair evaluation, not confirmation only.
}
